		{
			"id": "112000203553484795",
			"parentId": "111999873523772018",
			"url": "https://hj.9fs.net/khm/p/1708984994.366723",
			"author": {
				"name": "khm",
				"url": "https://hj.9fs.net/khm",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/111/935/114/538/278/240/original/250430e1cb03a1ff.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-26T22:03:14.000Z",
			"content": "Most of these features are aimed at driving 'engagement' or exfiltrating data off the user's device and into the vendor's analytics suite.  Getting rid of them is the first thing I've seen from Apple in approximately thirty years which I would consider a pro-user change.<br><br>Incidentally, the chart itself is kind of a gross psychological game, since \"Windows\" in fact means \"Edge\" which is of course just a badge-engineered Chrome rebrand.  So it's really comparing Google's platform, Google's other platform, and Apple, but it's calling one \"Windows\" to make it seem like there's some kind of majority opinion from which Apple is deviating, when in fact it's only Google software that supports all these dark patterns.<br><br>I'm not going to run out and buy a smartphone, or any other Apple hardware for that matter, but it's nice to know they're not entirely complicit in the ongoing personal-data gold-rush.<br><br>CC: <a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/users/zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@zachleat@zachleat.com</a><br>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
